    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         🔴 THE VALUE BASIS MOVED OFF THE DISPOSITION ON 2026-08-25 (user decision), AND THE OLD
            FORM MUST NOT COME BACK.
         Until this date the 50-point Value term divided by `Disposition__r.Asking_Price__c`
         (label "Expected Value"). `Asking_Price__c` is RETIRED from the disposition flow — the
         user removed it from the record page — and the basis is now the property asset's own
         `Target_Sale_Price__c`, TWO hops out: Disposition__r.Property_Asset__r.
         The field itself is NOT deleted (additive retirement, per this repo's standing rule); it
         is simply no longer read by anything on the disposition side. Do not "restore" it here.

         ⚠ THE BASIS IS STILL `Property_Asset__r.Target_Sale_Price__c` AFTER THE 2026-09-01
         RE-WEIGHTING BELOW. Decision A1 — NO FREEZE — is not revisited: the score keeps measuring
         against the LIVE target sale price, not a snapshot taken at Conversion 5.

         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         🔴 RE-WEIGHTED 2026-09-01 — TRANCHE 4 ITEM 1 (story 20), USER DECISION AT GATE 1.
            THE OLD WEIGHTING IS QUOTED RATHER THAN DELETED, BECAUSE IT IS THE THING A READER WILL
            OTHERWISE ASSUME IS STILL IN FORCE.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             RETIRED: Value 50 / Commission 25 / Speed 25 = 100. Three terms, no broker-quality
             input at all, and `Is_Buyer_Ready__c` / `Is_Known_Performer__c` were INERT — captured
             by Tranche 1 and read by nothing.

             IN FORCE: Value 40 / Commission 20 / Speed 20 / Buyer Ready 10 / Known Performer 10
             = 100.

         🔴 IT IS A REBALANCE, NOT A BONUS. The user rejected the "base + up to 20, clamped at 100"
         shape explicitly. A clamp compresses the top of the range and manufactures ties exactly
         among the best brokers — the one region where the score has to discriminate — and it would
         give the org a second definition of a number that is labelled, described and reported as
         0-100. Every term was rescaled instead, so the total is still exactly 100 with no cap
         distortion anywhere in the range.

         ⚠ THIS CHANGES EVERY EXISTING SCORE, INCLUDING ON ROWS WHERE NEITHER FLAG IS SET. A row
         with both flags clear now scores 80% of what it scored before (40/20/20 in place of
         50/25/25). That is arithmetic, not a defect, and it is why the change cannot be described
         as "adding two terms".

         ⚠ WHAT THE TWO FLAGS ARE WORTH, STATED SO THE BA CAN SEE THE LEVER THEY SIGNED OFF:
         20 of 100 points, against a 40-point Value term that spans bid/target from 0 to 110%. Two
         ticked checkboxes are therefore worth roughly a 55%-of-target swing in bid price — a
         Buyer-Ready, Known-Performing broker beats a stranger bidding materially more. Worked
         example (both at 2.5% commission, 30 days to market): a broker bidding 105% of target with
         neither flag scored 70.65 and WON under the old weights; a broker bidding 98% with both
         flags now scores 73.97 and wins. That inversion is the requested behaviour, and it is the
         reason the number — not just the shape — needed sign-off.

         🔴 A DEPLOY OF THIS FILE RE-RANKS READS INSTANTLY AND RE-RANKS NOTHING THAT IS STORED.
         `BOV_Score__c` is a formula, so the matrix, the Replace Broker picker labels, the
         `BOV_Tracker` report and every `ORDER BY BOV_Score__c` change the moment this lands.
         `Submission_Status__c` is STORED, and `BovAutoSelectionService.reselect` re-ranks a parent
         only on insert / delete / undelete, on one of its six change keys, or on a
         `Property_Asset__c.Target_Sale_Price__c` edit. A FORMULA DEPLOY IS NONE OF THOSE, and
         neither is a no-op re-save of a BOV row — which is worth stating explicitly, because
         "just touch the records" is the first thing anyone tries and it does nothing at all here.
         So an unlocked sale can sit with a new top scorer beside a stale "Selected" pill
         indefinitely.
         ⚠ NO BACKFILL SCRIPT HAS BEEN WRITTEN, AND ITS ABSENCE IS DELIBERATE AS OF 2026-09-01.
         The only in-contract repair is a one-off call to the PUBLIC
         `BovAutoSelectionService.reselect(rows, null)` — a null `priorById` means "always
         re-rank", per that method's own ApexDoc — chunked by disposition. That is a REAL DML
         operation on live records that re-appoints brokers, so it is an authorised business step,
         not a deploy step, and it was left for the user to commission rather than shipped as a
         file somebody could run by accident. Do not add one without saying so first.
         ✅ A sale locked by an approval (pending, approved OR rejected) or by
         `Is_Manually_Appointed__c` moves for nobody, including that script: the lock is evaluated
         across ALL siblings of the disposition, so a manually-appointed broker is fully protected
         from this re-weighting.

         ⚠ THE GUARD IS DELIBERATELY IDENTICAL IN SHAPE TO THE OLD ONE — blank OR <= 0 OR no
         BOV_Amount__c returns **null**, with formulaTreatBlanksAs = BlankAsBlank. That null means
         "NOT SCOREABLE", never "worst": `BovAutoSelectionService` skips null-scored rows entirely
         and `BovSubmissionSelector`'s `ORDER BY BOV_Score__c DESC NULLS LAST` only keeps them off
         the front of the list. Changing the guard to a 0 would silently make every unpriced sale
         auto-appoint whichever broker quoted first.

         🔴 THE TWO NEW TERMS SIT **INSIDE** THE GUARD, NOT AFTER IT, AND THAT PLACEMENT IS THE
         WHOLE GUARD. A row with no `BOV_Amount__c` but both flags ticked must still score NULL,
         not 20. Hoisting the two `IF(...)` terms outside the `IF(OR(...), null, ...)` wrapper
         would make an unpriced response rankable and defeat the guard from the other end — the
         same failure as changing the null to a 0, arrived at from a direction nobody is watching.

         ⚠ A CHECKBOX CANNOT EXPRESS "NOT YET ASSESSED", AND NO FORMULA SETTING FIXES THAT.
         `formulaTreatBlanksAs` does not interact with a Checkbox at all — a Checkbox on a saved
         row is never null — so an analyst who has not got round to assessing a broker penalises
         them by exactly as much as one who assessed them and said no. That is a BUSINESS
         consequence accepted with the weighting, not something `BlankAsZero` vs `BlankAsBlank`
         can change. Do not "fix" it by flipping that enum; the enum guards the VALUE terms.

         ⚠ `Hist_Success_Rate__c` IS STILL NOT IN THIS FORMULA, AND AFTER TRANCHE 4 THAT IS A
         SECOND DELIBERATE OMISSION RATHER THAN THE ORIGINAL ONE. It is captured on every
         submission and granted read in both disposition permission sets. Item 1 was scoped by the
         user to the two Booleans only. If it is ever folded in, it is a third re-weighting and
         every sentence above applies again.

         ⚠ A NULL `Property_Asset__c` LOOKUP ON THE DISPOSITION IS COVERED BY THE SAME `ISBLANK`.
         The lookup is not required (deleteConstraint SetNull), so the whole chain can be null;
         ISBLANK over a broken chain is TRUE, so such a row scores null rather than erroring.

         ⚠ CROSS-OBJECT COST: two relationship hops, well inside the platform's limits, and
         formula fields compute in SYSTEM context so the FLS on Target_Sale_Price__c does not
         gate this field. The USER_MODE FLS question applies only to the SOQL that reads the same
         value — see DispositionSelector.selectApprovalContextById.
         ⚠ THE COMPILED-FORMULA CAP IS 3,900 CHARACTERS AND IS NOT COMPUTABLE FROM SOURCE. Budgeted
         rather than measured: the two added terms are SAME-OBJECT Checkbox references, so they add
         ZERO relationship expansion and the count of distinct cross-object paths is UNCHANGED at
         one (`Disposition__r.Property_Asset__r`, already traversed twice). The failure direction is
         loud — a deploy error, never a wrong number — which is why this is budgeted here and
         verified by the deploy rather than asserted.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <fullName>BOV_Score__c</fullName>
    <description>Composite BOV ranking score, 0-100. Value 40 pts (BOV Amount vs the Disposition&apos;s Property Asset Target Sale Price), Commission 20 pts (inverse), Speed 20 pts (inverse days to market), Buyer Ready 10 pts, Known Performer 10 pts. Re-weighted 2026-09-01 from 50/25/25 - every existing score changed, including on rows where neither flag is set. Blank when the asset has no Target Sale Price (or it is zero or less), or the BOV has no amount - the two flags cannot make an unpriced response scoreable.</description>
    <externalId>false</externalId>
    <formula>IF(
    OR(
        ISBLANK(Disposition__r.Property_Asset__r.Target_Sale_Price__c),
        Disposition__r.Property_Asset__r.Target_Sale_Price__c &lt;= 0,
        ISBLANK(BOV_Amount__c)
    ),
    null,
    (40 * MIN(BOV_Amount__c / Disposition__r.Property_Asset__r.Target_Sale_Price__c, 1.10) / 1.10)
    + IF(ISBLANK(Commission_Rate__c), 0, 20 * (3 - MAX(1, MIN(3, Commission_Rate__c * 100))) / 2)
    + IF(ISBLANK(Days_To_Market__c), 0, 20 * (60 - MAX(15, MIN(60, Days_To_Market__c))) / 45)
    + IF(Is_Buyer_Ready__c, 10, 0)
    + IF(Is_Known_Performer__c, 10, 0)
)</formula>
    <formulaTreatBlanksAs>BlankAsBlank</formulaTreatBlanksAs>
    <inlineHelpText>Auto-calculated, 0-100: Value 40, Commission 20, Speed 20, Buyer Ready 10, Known Performer 10. The value part is scored against the property asset&apos;s Target Sale Price - if that is blank or zero this score stays blank even when both flags are ticked, and a blank score is not a rank, so the response cannot be auto-selected.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>BOV Score</label>
    <precision>5</precision>
    <required>false</required>
    <scale>2</scale>
    <trackHistory>false</trackHistory>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
    <type>Number</type>
    <unique>false</unique>
</CustomField>
